  "response": "I’m using the repository’s crimes risk workflow to interpret the targeted finding and keep the recommendation scoped to the blocking I/O.\n\nThe culprit is finding `crime_00008`, “Sync I/O in Hot Path.” Its evidence is:\n\n> \"`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)\"  \n> \"lines: 12, 13, 15\"  \n> \"swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it\"\n\nThe blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`. Each blocks the event loop per request.\n\nThe smallest robust migration is to use `node:fs/promises`, eliminate the redundant existence check, and treat `ENOENT` from `stat()` as the missing-file case:\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile, stat } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  let metadata;\n\n  try {\n    metadata = await stat(SCHEMA_PATH);\n  } catch (error) {\n    if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === \"ENOENT\") return null;\n    throw error;\n  }\n\n  if (metadata.size === 0) return null;\n\n  return JSON.parse(await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\"));\n}\n```\n\nThis preserves the exported name, parameters, and resolved values (`null` or parsed JSON). The exact synchronous return type `unknown` cannot be preserved while making filesystem access nonblocking; it must become `Promise<unknown>`. If the public request handler is already async, keep that handler’s signature unchanged and simply use:\n\n```ts\nconst schema = await loadPlanSchema();\n```\n\nNo cache is needed—the filesystem operations themselves are moved off the event loop.",
